  "narrative": "There are no assessments due at this explore stage, so no existing claim is being reassessed now. The accepted, independently replicated dataset-specific core remains: MSI-high is associated with much stronger WRN dependency (H11); SMARCA4 loss is associated with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency (H12); TP53 loss is associated with weaker USP7 dependency (H48); and KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency (H13). Each accepted core effect has discovery and validation estimates near 0.8 in signed form, with lower bounds far above the practical absolute dependency-score benchmark and far above any plausible 10-percent interpretation in arbitrary units. The scientific question is now less about whether these associations exist in the supplied dataset and more about how specific, robust, and context-limited they are.\n\nThe first proposed refinements ask whether two core associations persist in high-Cas9 screens, a measured screen-quality stratum. The WRN MSI-high comparison and the USP7 TP53-loss comparison are tested directly among screens with cas9_activity_pct >= 75%. Expected directions are the same as the overall core claims: MSI-high should show more negative WRN scores, and TP53-loss should show less negative USP7 scores. Large, precisely estimated effects in this stratum would support robustness against Cas9-performance dependence. Small or attenuated effects would be important because they would indicate that the high-level interactions previously tested were not sufficient to rule out a quality-dependent context restriction. I will accept only effects whose intervals remain clearly above the practical ~0.1 dependency-score benchmark.\n\nThe next set is a specificity battery for the large WRN and SMARCA2 dependencies. BRCA2 loss and TP53 loss are tested against WRN and SMARCA2 dependency. For each, the claim direction is negative, meaning that if the exposure were broadly associated with dependency it would show a more negative score and therefore stronger dependency. I expect these comparisons to be small or null because WRN has previously appeared relatively selective for MSI-high, and SMARCA2 appears tied to SMARCA4 loss. If BRCA2 loss or TP53 loss produced effects near 0.1 or larger, that would substantially weaken claims of biological specificity and suggest broader DNA-repair or tumor-suppressor-context dependence. This is therefore a falsifiable control rather than a new positive claim.\n\nThe MTAP comparisons address co-deletion biology around p53 pathway outcomes. The USP7 association is already supported overall, in adherent culture, adenocarcinoma, the Ky library, and MTAP-loss cells. I now test TP53 loss against USP7 dependency restricted to MTAP-WT cells. This checks whether the USP7 association is merely tied to 9p21-region deletion co-alterations or remains meaningful when MTAP deletion is absent. A positive raw contrast would mean less negative USP7 dependency in TP53-loss cells, i.e. weaker dependency, as accepted for H48. For MDM2, TP53 loss restricted to MTAP-WT cells tests whether p53-MDM2 biology is clearer when MTAP co-deletion is not present. The expected direction is again positive for weaker MDM2 dependency in TP53-loss cells, but prior main-effect evidence was near zero, so this is a targeted mechanistic-context check rather than a strong positive expectation.\n\nFinally, I register two RAS/PI3K/receptor crosstalk interactions. The KRAS interaction tests whether KRAS mutation effects on KRAS dependency differ in NF1-loss cells. NF1 loss activates RAS signaling, so the effect could be enhanced or redundant; I leave the raw direction uncertain, with a negative claim direction for a possible stronger KRAS dependency. The EGFR interaction tests PIK3CA mutation as a downstream bypass modifier of EGFR-amplification effects on EGFR dependency. The claim direction is positive because downstream PI3K activation would be expected to make the EGFR-amplification contrast less negative, i.e. attenuated upstream receptor dependence. Because prior EGFR amplification main effects were unstable, this is exploratory pathway-redundancy testing, not a positive biological claim. All proposed comparisons are associations within the supplied dataset and validation structure, not causal driver statements or externally generalizable clinical biomarker claims.",
  "research_notes": "Current accepted core, with discovery and validation support: H11 MSI-high -> stronger WRN; H12 SMARCA4-loss -> stronger SMARCA2; H48 TP53-loss -> weaker USP7; H13 KEAP1-loss -> weaker NFE2L2. Each signed effect near 0.8 and lower bounds far above the practical ~0.1 dependency benchmark. H31 adherent-culture MSI interaction failed validation and is rejected; H55 MYC/KIF18A failed validation and is rejected. Many modifier checks (Cas9 high interactions, KEAP1 specificity, co-alteration, MYC, MSI-DHX9) were small/null and support robustness/specificity of the core.\n\nNew exploration priorities: 1) direct core effects in high Cas9 screens for WRN and USP7 to test measured screen-quality robustness; 2) specificity controls for WRN and SMARCA2 using BRCA2 loss and TP53 loss, expected null unless broad DNA-repair/tumor-suppressor context effects exist; 3) MTAP-WT direct effects for USP7 and MDM2 to test whether p53 pathway associations are confounded by MTAP/CDKN2A co-deletion; 4) KRAS/NF1 and EGFR/PIK3CA pathway crosstalk interactions. Continue accepting only large intervals excluding trivial effects. Interpret all findings as dataset-specific associations, not causal mechanisms or generalizable clinical claims. If the new specificity battery yields nulls, remaining active low-priority pathway and metadata checks should be deferred or closed unless they address a clear biological question."
}

