  "narrative": "Synthesis report after iteration 10. All statements are associations among measured variables in the supplied lung-adenocarcinoma CRISPR-screen dataset. They are not causal claims, and generalizability to other screens, tissues, or clinical populations remains uncertain.\n\nValidation and response-deadline assessments:\n- H75: The independent validation R105 is concordant with discovery R96 for the CDKN2A-loss / MDM2 dependency claim and gives a very large, precise effect on the continuous MDM2 dependency scale. I accept and close H75. Scientifically, CDKN2A loss is associated with stronger MDM2 dependency in this dataset, and this is supported not only by the overall claim but also by context-specific accepted claims: TP53-WT cells, high-Cas9 cells, and low 9p21 copy-number cells.\n- H39: The independent validation R106 confirms the MSI-high / WRN dependency association within adherent culture. Discovery and validation estimates are both very large relative to the WRN score distribution and exclude the null. I accept and close H39. This strengthens confidence that the MSI-high / WRN association is not restricted to the full eligible population and survives a culture-context check.\n- H128: Discovery suggested that CDKN2A loss was associated with stronger MDM2 dependency specifically in TP53-WT, MTAP-loss cells. Independent validation was much weaker and imprecise, with an interval crossing the null and a point estimate below the magnitude I had regarded as reliable in this scale. I therefore do not accept a clinically meaningful MTAP-loss-specific CDKN2A effect. H128 is unresolved and deferred. This is not a positive MTAP-specific claim; it remains an underpowered or possibly false-positive subgroup result. Importantly, H136 showed no MTAP-only effect among CDKN2A-WT low-9p21 cells, which argues against a simple MTAP / 9p21 co-deletion proxy explanation.\n\nSupported dataset-specific findings:\n1. MSI-high cells show stronger WRN dependency (more negative CRISPR dependency scores). This is one of the strongest and most robust findings. It is supported overall and in adenocarcinoma, high-Cas9, higher mutation burden, batch_a, and adherent-culture checks; independent validation confirms both the main and adherent-subgroup results.\n2. SMARCA4 loss is associated with stronger SMARCA2 dependency. This is also very large and robust across adenocarcinoma, high-Cas9, batch_a, culture checks, ARID1A-loss cells, and EMT-high cells. The independent validation of the overall claim strengthens this association.\n3. KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency, indicated by less negative / more positive NFE2L2 scores in KEAP1-loss cells. The direction and magnitude are robust across TP53-WT, high-Cas9, adherent, batch_a, adenocarcinoma, and other context checks, with independent validation of the overall claim.\n4. TP53 loss is associated with weaker USP7 dependency, i.e. less negative USP7 scores in TP53-loss cells. This is robust across adenocarcinoma, MTAP-stratified, high-Cas9, CDKN2A-stratified, and batch checks, with independent validation of the overall claim.\n5. CDKN2A loss is associated with stronger MDM2 dependency. The overall claim is validated. The TP53-WT subset and high-Cas9 subset are also accepted, and the low-9p21 copy-number subset is precise and clinically substantial in this dataset. However, the specificity of this association relative to 9p21 copy number and MTAP deletion is not fully resolved.\n\nNegative or unsupported findings:\n- The direct oncogene / matched dependency pairings are generally not supported in the tested directions: KRAS mutation and dependency_KRAS, BRAF V600E and dependency_BRAF, EGFR amplification and dependency_EGFR, and ERBB2 amplification and dependency_ERBB2 are rejected or null. Many adjacent receptor / pathway checks were also weak, null, or deliberately deferred.\n- MYC amplification, PTEN loss, BRCA2 loss, RB1 loss, STK11 loss, NF1 loss, PIK3CA mutation, APC mutation, SMAD4 loss, and the constructed research markers generally do not explain the dependency outcomes in the tested contrasts. H104, BRCA2 loss with SMARCA2 dependency, failed independent validation and is rejected.\n- MTAP loss alone does not robustly explain USP7 or MDM2 dependency. The MTAP-only check in low-9p21 CDKN2A-WT cells, H136, is null and supports the conclusion that MTAP loss by itself is not the primary driver of the accepted CDKN2A-MDM2 association.\n- The culture-type interaction for WRN, H31, was not replicated and is rejected. Research-marker interactions for WRN, SMARCA2, and NFE2L2 were generally unsupported.\n\nUnresolved uncertainty:\n- The main unresolved scientific boundary is the CDKN2A-MDM2 association: is it primarily a CDKN2A / MDM2 biological association, or is it partly a 9p21 copy-number / co-deletion context marker? The high 9p21 copy-number partition cannot be tested because there is essentially no CDKN2A-loss exposure in that stratum. The MTAP-stratified low-9p21 tests are the most informative next evidence, but they are still not yet available. H128 and H137 remain too uncertain to claim that MTAP loss amplifies the CDKN2A effect.\n- Several deferred receptor / PI3K hypotheses remain unanalyzed, but they are lower priority because the receptor/pathway results tested so far have been repeatedly negative or unstable.\n- Biological interpretation remains uncertain. Dependency scores are CRISPR screen measurements, and associations may reflect true tumor biology, copy-number / locus effects, screen-library or assay context, or correlations among genomic features.\n\nMost informative next analysis:\nThe highest-value next work is CDKN2A-MDM2 specificity. H141 and H142, already registered, should be prioritized: test CDKN2A-loss versus CDKN2A-WT within low 9p21 copy-number cells, separately in MTAP-WT and MTAP-loss strata. If the CDKN2A-MDM2 association persists in MTAP-WT low-9p21 cells, it favors a broader 9p21 / CDKN2A locus association. If it is strong only in MTAP-loss cells, it favors a co-deletion context. The newly registered interaction proposal asks directly whether MTAP loss amplifies the CDKN2A-MDM2 effect within low-9p21 cells. These analyses should decide whether the accepted CDKN2A-MDM2 association should remain broad and low-9p21-specific or be refined to an MTAP-loss-specific claim. No further independent validation requests remain in this run, so the next analyses should focus on direct subgroup partitioning rather than additional broad screening.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 10 synthesis: accepted validated associations include MSI-high / stronger WRN dependency, SMARCA4-loss / stronger SMARCA2 dependency, KEAP1-loss / weaker NFE2L2 dependency, TP53-loss / weaker USP7 dependency, and CDKN2A-loss / stronger MDM2 dependency overall. The CDKN2A-MDM2 association is also accepted in TP53-WT, high-Cas9, and low 9p21 copy-number contexts. H39 MSI-high / WRN in adherent culture is accepted after validation. H128 (CDKN2A effect in TP53-WT MTAP-loss cells) was validated but not confirmed and remains unresolved/deferred. H136 rejects MTAP-only effects in low-9p21 CDKN2A-WT cells, disfavoring a simple MTAP/9p21 proxy. H141 and H142 are active and should be analyzed next: CDKN2A effect within low-9p21 cells split by MTAP loss. New low-9p21 CDKN2A-by-MTAP interaction proposal registered. Continue deprioritizing receptor/PI3K, MYC, PTEN, BRCA2, and constructed-marker checks. All claims remain dataset-specific associations, not causal or generalizable."
}

