  "narrative": "No assessments were due. I closed several superseded unresolved deferred receptor/pathway claims as low-priority dataset-only branches: overall RIT1-EGFR amplification, overall TMED10-EGFR amplification, overall KRAS-BRAF V600E, overall TMED10-ERBB2 amplification, overall KRAS-NF1 loss, and overall EGFR-ERBB2 amplification. These are not rejections from evidence; they are closed to make room for cleaner molecular-context tests. I registered twelve new comparisons. Four test direct-driver dependency in cleaner molecular contexts excluding co-occurring KRAS/BRAF/EGFR/ERBB2 drivers: KRAS mutation with KRAS dependency, BRAF V600E with BRAF dependency, EGFR amplification with EGFR dependency, and ERBB2 amplification with ERBB2 dependency. These are refinements of earlier overall tests and therefore include the earlier direct results as motivating evidence. Two test cross-receptor specificity: EGFR amplification with ERBB2 dependency in KRAS/BRAF/ERBB2-amplification-free cells, and ERBB2 amplification with EGFR dependency in KRAS/BRAF/EGFR-amplification-free cells. Four test downstream signaling or pathway context: KRAS mutation, EGFR amplification, and ERBB2 amplification with STAT3 dependency in clean contexts, plus KRAS mutation with RIT1 dependency in a clean context. One tests NF1 loss in KRAS/BRAF/EGFR/ERBB2-driver-free cells as an indirect RAS-pathway activation exposure for KRAS dependency. Finally, I refine the accepted SMARCA4-loss association with SMARCA2 dependency to ARID1A-wildtype cells using the accepted discovery and validation evidence as motivation, testing whether the SMARCA4 effect is not merely an ARID1A-loss context. Direction conventions are preserved: for these new proposals direction -1 means a positive signed estimate corresponds to a more negative, stronger dependency score in the exposed group. Direct-driver hypotheses anticipate raw negative contrasts, while cross-driver and downstream hypotheses are registered as uncertain direction. Validation budget remains exhausted, so all new work is dataset-specific association analysis, not causal inference or generalizable clinical prediction.",
