  "narrative": "The clean molecular-context analyses do not support direct driver-gene dependency claims or downstream STAT3/RIT1/NF1 associations. H172-H175 test KRAS, BRAF, EGFR, and ERBB2 alterations against their matched dependency outcomes in cells without co-occurring receptor/driver alterations. All estimates are close to null or in the registered opposite direction: H172 KRAS dependency is near zero (+0.009, CI -0.011 to +0.030), H173 BRAF dependency is small and opposite expected (-0.030, CI -0.064 to +0.004), H174 EGFR dependency is imprecise and below the 10% threshold (+0.022, CI -0.013 to +0.056), and H175 ERBB2 dependency is negligible (-0.007, CI -0.045 to +0.032). Cross-receptor specificity checks H176-H177 are also small and imprecise. These null/near-null patterns are consistent with prior overall tests and argue against accepting any of these direct or cross-driver hypotheses. H178-H182 test downstream signaling and RAS-pathway contexts: KRAS mutation and EGFR amplification with STAT3 dependency, ERBB2 amplification with STAT3 dependency, NF1 loss with KRAS dependency, and KRAS mutation with RIT1 dependency. None support the registered stronger-dependency claims. H179 is the only interval that excludes zero, but it is opposite the registered claim: EGFR amplification is associated with weaker STAT3 dependency (signed estimate -0.048, CI -0.095 to -0.001). The effect is extremely close to the null boundary, likely falls below reliable clinical significance when considering the full interval, is not validated, and is inconsistent with the broader pattern of imprecise or null EGFR-STAT3 tests, including H5. Given the large multiplicity across the run, this is better treated as a boundary/multiplicity artifact than as a new supported reversal, so H179 is rejected rather than pursued. H180 and H181 are imprecise and near null; H182 is also negligible. In contrast, H183 strongly supports the accepted SMARCA4-loss/SMARCA2-dependency association even after excluding ARID1A-loss cells: signed estimate +0.776 (CI +0.754 to +0.798), n 296 vs 1129. This is a very large effect relative to the SMARCA2 score scale and materially reinforces predictor and outcome specificity, though it remains an association in the supplied dataset and does not establish causality. Overall, these analyses close the clean-context direct-driver and downstream branches, strengthen the robustness of the core accepted associations, and do not introduce new accepted findings.",
