  "narrative": "No assessments were due, so no claim status changes are made in this explore form. The validation request budget is exhausted, so the remaining work is dataset-specific association analysis rather than new independent validation or causal inference. I register twelve targeted comparisons to test robustness and context-dependence of the accepted core predictor-outcome associations: TP53 loss with USP7, KEAP1 loss with NFE2L2, SMARCA4 loss with SMARCA2, and CDKN2A loss with MDM2. These are refinements of accepted parent claims rather than new random subgroup explorations. Four tests examine assay/library robustness in Ky or Humagne-CD libraries, especially where Ky library-specific effects had already been reported for some core outcomes but not others. Four tests examine high mutation burden (mutation_burden >= 7) as a genomic-instability context. Two interaction tests examine whether TP53-loss/USP7 and CDKN2A-loss/MDM2 effects differ between adenocarcinoma and the non-adenocarcinoma complement, using lineage subtype to probe broader lung subtype context. One test refines the accepted CDKN2A-loss/MDM2 association into severe low 9p21 copy number (copy_number_9p21 <= -0.5225, approximately the lower quartile), to ask whether the CDKN2A/MDM2 association is stronger when the 9p21 locus is more deeply deleted. For the mean_difference refinements, the anticipated raw direction follows the accepted parent effect: TP53-loss/USP7 and KEAP1-loss/NFE2L2 anticipate positive raw exposed-minus-comparator contrasts because the accepted effect is weaker dependency, whereas SMARCA4-loss/SMARCA2 and CDKN2A-loss/MDM2 anticipate negative raw contrasts because the accepted effect is stronger dependency. For interaction tests, direction is set to the parent main-effect sign, but anticipated_direction is zero because the purpose is to test whether the adenocarcinoma effect differs from the complement rather than to pre-assert an effect modification. The MSI-high/WRN association is not repeated in rare library or high-TMB contexts here because its already-supported contexts are sufficient and MSI prevalence makes some rare-library intersections underpowered. These proposed analyses remain observational within the supplied dataset and do not establish causality, rescue, or external generalizability.",
  "research_notes": "CURRENT NOTEBOOK. No validation budget remains; no response-deadline assessments were due. Core accepted dataset-specific associations remain: MSI-high with stronger WRN dependency; SMARCA4 loss with stronger SMARCA2 dependency; KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2 dependency; TP53 loss with weaker USP7 dependency; CDKN2A loss with stronger MDM2 dependency. These are associations in the supplied lung cell-line CRISPR dataset, not causal or generalizable clinical claims. Direct oncogene/receptor/pathway predictors, many genomic markers, constructed research markers, and most interactions are rejected, null, near null, or closed as low-priority dataset-only branches. Current plan: stop broad random subgroup splits and use only targeted robustness/refinement branches with prior scientific rationale: library contexts (Ky, Humagne-CD), genomic-instability context (high mutation burden >= 7), severe 9p21 deletion context for CDKN2A/MDM2, and lineage-subtype interactions for core associations where not already tested. Humagne-CD intersections may be underpowered for rare exposures; if invalid, close rather than interpret. Highest-value next work remains external replication and mechanistic perturbation/rescue, not further post-hoc within-dataset splitting. If restricted to this dataset, prefer pre-specified robustness contexts and rare-stratum population over arbitrary thresholds."
}

