  "narrative": "I interpreted all returned estimates as already signed to the registered direction. For direction -1 mean-difference claims, a positive signed estimate means the exposed group has a more negative, stronger dependency score; for direction +1 claims, a positive signed estimate means the exposed group has a less negative, weaker dependency score; for interaction claims, a positive signed estimate means the registered exposed-minus-comparator contrast is stronger in the named subgroup than in its complement. H268 accepts as a within-dataset PTEN-loss/STAT3-dependency association in PIK3CA-mutant cells: the signed difference is +0.261, 95% CI 0.118 to 0.403, with 36 exposed and 273 comparator cells. The effect is large relative to the STAT3 dependency distribution, but the exposed cell count is modest and the subgroup was tested after selection on PIK3CA state, so I keep it deferred rather than closed because PTEN-STAT3 remains discovery-only and may be confounded by PI3K-pathway and genomic context. H269 similarly accepts a PTEN-loss/STAT3 association among TP53-loss cells: +0.198, 95% CI 0.089 to 0.307, n 52/309. This is somewhat smaller than the main PTEN effect but still imprecisely positive and biologically plausible; it remains deferred as an unvalidated within-dataset context check. H270 rejects the claim of a positive high-aneuploidy interaction modifying PTEN-loss/STAT3 dependency: the signed interaction is -0.089, 95% CI -0.215 to 0.037. The point estimate is opposite the registered direction and the interval is too wide to support a meaningful positive interaction. This is not a claim that high aneuploidy has no effect; rather, this dataset provides no evidence for the specific claimed positive modification. H271 accepts a large PTEN-loss/STAT3 association in high IFN-gamma signature cells: +0.458, 95% CI 0.345 to 0.570, n 59/384. The magnitude is much larger than the overall PTEN-STAT3 effect and the interval excludes zero comfortably. However, the subgroup was defined by a continuous assay and PTEN loss may co-occur with inflammatory or lineage states, so I accept it as an internally supported but deferred context finding, not a causal or generalized result. H272 rejects ARID1A-loss association with SMARCA2 dependency among SMARCA4-intact cells: +0.002, 95% CI -0.021 to 0.026. This is essentially null and inconsistent with the paralog-compensation hypothesis. H273-H277 are accepted as confirmatory context checks of previously supported core associations. H273 shows TP53 loss is associated with weaker USP7 dependency among KEAP1-loss cells: +0.791, 95% CI 0.750 to 0.832, n 74/276. H274 shows KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency among TP53-loss cells: +0.828, 95% CI 0.778 to 0.878, n 74/287. H275 shows MSI-high cells have stronger WRN dependency among faster-growing cells: +0.776, 95% CI 0.733 to 0.820, n 58/719. H276 shows CDKN2A loss is associated with stronger MDM2 dependency among STK11-loss cells: +0.111, 95% CI 0.038 to 0.184, n 63/276; the interval is less expansive than the main validated CDKN2A-MDM2 effect but remains positive and consistent. H277 shows SMARCA4 loss is associated with stronger SMARCA2 dependency in high-stemness cells: +0.789, 95% CI 0.749 to 0.830, n 91/362. These effects are large, precise, and consistent with earlier accepted and validated findings, though they remain associations within overlapping discovery samples. H278 rejects MYC amplification as a predictor of stronger STAT3 dependency: -0.008, 95% CI -0.040 to 0.025, with a null-to-negligible signed effect. H279 rejects STK11 loss as a predictor of stronger STAT3 dependency: +0.017, 95% CI -0.013 to 0.048; the point estimate is small, imprecise, and crosses zero. No validation request is made because the remaining voluntary independent-validation budget is zero. Overall, this appraise supports context-specific extensions of PTEN-STAT3 as within-dataset associations, confirms core accepted dependencies 